## 1. Common preparation (both methods)

### 1.1 Point DNS at the server
Create an `A` record `collection.example.com → <server public IP>`. You need it
before requesting HTTPS certificates.

### 1.2 Get the code onto the server
```bash
sudo mkdir -p /opt/saypy-collection
sudo chown $USER:$USER /opt/saypy-collection
git clone <your-repo-url> /opt/saypy-collection
cd /opt/saypy-collection
git checkout main          # or the branch/tag you want to deploy
```

> `.env`, `.env.dev`, `.env.prod`, `api/.env` and `.secrets.key` are **not** in
> git. You create them on the server in the steps below. Never copy the dev
> `.secrets.key` to production.

### 1.3 PostgreSQL: create the database and user

If Postgres is not installed yet (and you aren't using a managed DB such as RDS
or DigitalOcean):
```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y postgresql postgresql-contrib
sudo systemctl enable --now postgresql
```

Create a dedicated user and database:
```bash
sudo -u postgres psql <<'SQL'
CREATE USER saypy WITH PASSWORD 'STRONG_DB_PASSWORD';
CREATE DATABASE saypy_core OWNER saypy;
GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON DATABASE saypy_core TO saypy;
SQL
```

The resulting connection string (note the `+psycopg` driver):
```
postgresql+psycopg://saypy:STRONG_DB_PASSWORD@localhost:5432/saypy_core
```

> **Moving existing data from dev?** Dump from the dev cluster and restore into prod:
> ```bash
> # on the dev machine (dev Postgres runs on port 5433)
> pg_dump -h localhost -p 5433 -U postgres -Fc saypy_core > saypy_core.dump
> # copy to the server, then:
> pg_restore -h localhost -U saypy -d saypy_core --no-owner saypy_core.dump
> ```
> Also copy `api/uploads/` across so existing evidence files still resolve.

### 1.4 Generate production secrets

```bash
# A random JWT signing key
python3 -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(64))"
```

Optional: **encrypt** the secrets so they aren't stored in plain text in env files:
```bash
cd /opt/saypy-collection/api
python3 -m venv /tmp/saypy-secrets && /tmp/saypy-secrets/bin/pip install -q cryptography
# 1. New key for THIS environment (saved at the repo root)
/tmp/saypy-secrets/bin/python manage_secrets.py genkey > ../.secrets.key
chmod 600 ../.secrets.key
# 2. Encrypt each secret and paste the enc:... output into the env file
/tmp/saypy-secrets/bin/python manage_secrets.py encrypt "postgresql+psycopg://saypy:STRONG_DB_PASSWORD@localhost:5432/saypy_core"
/tmp/saypy-secrets/bin/python manage_secrets.py encrypt "<your JWT secret>"
/tmp/saypy-secrets/bin/python manage_secrets.py encrypt "<initial admin password>"
```
(For Method A you can run these commands with the app's own venv from step A.3
instead.) Keep a copy of `.secrets.key` somewhere safe, such as a password
manager. If you lose it, the `enc:` values can't be decrypted.

