# METHOD B — Deploy WITH Docker

The repo ships `docker-compose.prod.yml`:

- **backend**: the `api/Dockerfile` image, 4 uvicorn workers, published on
  `8001`. Its entrypoint waits for the DB, then runs `alembic upgrade head`,
  then starts uvicorn.
- **frontend**: a multi-stage build (Node builds `dist/`, then nginx serves it),
  published on `8080`.
- **No Postgres container.** The stack connects to an external Postgres through
  `DATABASE_URL`.
- `./.secrets.key` and `./api/uploads` are bind-mounted, so the key never goes
  into an image and uploads survive rebuilds.

## B.1 Install Docker Engine + Compose plugin

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y ca-certificates curl
sudo install -m 0755 -d /etc/apt/keyrings
sudo curl -fsSL https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu/gpg -o /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.asc
echo "deb [arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture) signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/docker.asc] \
https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu $(. /etc/os-release && echo $VERSION_CODENAME) stable" \
  | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list > /dev/null
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io docker-buildx-plugin docker-compose-plugin

sudo usermod -aG docker $USER   # log out and back in afterwards
docker compose version
```

## B.2 Get the code and create `.env.prod`

Follow sections 1.2 to 1.4 first (code, database, secrets). Then:

```bash
cd /opt/saypy-collection
cp .env.example .env.prod
nano .env.prod
chmod 600 .env.prod
```

```ini
# Postgres on the SAME host as Docker → use host.docker.internal (see B.3)
# Managed/remote Postgres → use its real hostname
DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://saypy:STRONG_DB_PASSWORD@host.docker.internal:5432/saypy_core
# or enc:...

JWT_SECRET_KEY=<long random value>
JWT_ALGORITHM=HS256
JWT_EXPIRE_MINUTES=480

CORS_ORIGINS=https://collection.example.com

SEED_ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD=<strong initial password>
SEED_CHURCH_NAMES=Kibada,KCC,Mwera,Goroka

# Build-time values for the frontend image (see section 0)
VITE_API_BASE_URL=https://collection.example.com/api
VITE_MEMBERS_API_BASE_URL=https://collection.example.com/api
```

**`.secrets.key` must exist at the repo root**, even if you don't use `enc:`
values, because compose bind-mounts it and the mount fails if the file is
missing. Either generate one (section 1.4) or `touch .secrets.key`.

```bash
mkdir -p api/uploads
```

## B.3 Let the containers reach Postgres

**Managed or remote Postgres:** make sure its firewall allows the server's IP,
and you're done.

**Postgres on the same host:** containers can't reach `127.0.0.1` on the host.
The compose file maps `host.docker.internal` to the host, but Postgres must
also listen on the Docker bridge and accept connections from it:

```bash
# Find your Postgres version directory
ls /etc/postgresql/
sudo nano /etc/postgresql/<ver>/main/postgresql.conf
#   listen_addresses = 'localhost,172.17.0.1'
#   (or '*' — fine as long as the firewall blocks 5432 from outside)

sudo nano /etc/postgresql/<ver>/main/pg_hba.conf
#   add:
#   host  saypy_core  saypy  172.16.0.0/12  scram-sha-256

sudo systemctl restart postgresql
```
`172.16.0.0/12` covers Docker's default bridge networks. Check yours with
`docker network inspect saypy-collection_default` once the stack has been
created. Keep `5432` closed in ufw.

## B.4 Keep container ports private (important)

Docker publishes ports **around ufw**: `"8001:8001"` and `"8080:80"` become
reachable from the internet even if ufw blocks them. Since a host nginx will
sit in front (B.6), bind both ports to localhost by editing
`docker-compose.prod.yml`:

```yaml
  backend:
    ports:
      - "127.0.0.1:8001:8001"
  ...
  frontend:
    ports:
      - "127.0.0.1:8080:80"
```

## B.5 Build and start

```bash
cd /opt/saypy-collection
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml --env-file .env.prod up -d --build
```

Check:
```bash
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml ps
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml logs -f backend    # look for "Running database migrations..." then uvicorn startup
curl http://127.0.0.1:8001/health
curl -I http://127.0.0.1:8080
```

Both services use `restart: always`, so they come back after a reboot as long
as Docker itself is enabled (`sudo systemctl enable docker`, which is the
default).

## B.6 Host nginx + HTTPS in front of the containers

```bash
sudo apt install -y nginx certbot python3-certbot-nginx
```

`/etc/nginx/sites-available/saypy`:
```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name collection.example.com;
    client_max_body_size 25M;

    location /api/ {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8001/;     # trailing slash strips /api
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Host              $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP         $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For   $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
        proxy_read_timeout 300s;
    }

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080;      # frontend container (nginx + SPA)
        proxy_set_header Host              $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
    }
}
```

```bash
sudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/saypy /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/
sudo rm -f /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/default
sudo nginx -t && sudo systemctl reload nginx
sudo certbot --nginx -d collection.example.com --redirect

sudo ufw allow OpenSSH
sudo ufw allow 'Nginx Full'
sudo ufw enable
```

Then run the checks in **A.11** (health, login, change the admin password, deep
link, upload).

> **No domain / LAN only?** Skip B.4 and B.6, leave `VITE_*` empty, and open
> ports 8080 and 8001. Users browse to `http://<server-ip>:8080`, and the
> frontend calls `http://<server-ip>:8001` automatically.

## B.7 Day-to-day operations

```bash
cd /opt/saypy-collection
DC="docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml --env-file .env.prod"

# Update / redeploy (migrations run automatically on backend start)
git pull
$DC up -d --build

# Logs
$DC logs -f backend
$DC logs -f frontend

# Shell / one-off commands in the backend
$DC exec backend alembic current
$DC exec backend alembic upgrade head
$DC exec backend python manage_secrets.py encrypt "..."

# Restart / stop
$DC restart backend
$DC down

# Reclaim disk from old images after several deploys
docker image prune -f
```

> Changed `VITE_API_BASE_URL` or `VITE_MEMBERS_API_BASE_URL`? Those are baked
> into the frontend image, so rebuild it:
> `$DC build --no-cache frontend && $DC up -d frontend`.

**Rollback:** `git checkout <previous-tag-or-commit>` then `$DC up -d --build`.
Be aware that Alembic migrations don't roll back on their own. If the new
release added a migration, run `$DC exec backend alembic downgrade -1` (or
restore a DB backup) **before** you check out the older code.

